<h1>John Kitover</h1>
<p>Businessman and Army National Guard veteran. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>A self-described Republican-Libertarian businessman, podcast host, and National Guard veteran who ran for Congress in the 16th District in 2026. Filed on the last day.</p>
<p><a href="https://johnkitover45.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (johnkitover45.com)</a></p>
<h2>What sets them apart</h2>
<p>Only Kitover: Would put city bankruptcy on the table to deal with the pension debt.</p>
<p>Property taxes: Freeze the levy in the first year, and no new taxes until Springfield reforms pensions. Holberg and Kelly would also freeze or lower them.</p>
<p>Not published: Nothing on housing, schools, or transit.</p>
<h2>Where they split from the field</h2>
<p>Property taxes: Freeze or lower them, with Kelly and Holberg. Hold the city&#39;s levy flat: Giannoulias. An increase is not off the table: Nee.</p>
<p>New taxes: No new taxes, with Kelly. Make the wealthy pay more: Johnson and Giannoulias. Cut taxes: Wilson. Savings first, before any new revenue: Quigley.</p>
<p>Pensions: Bankruptcy on the table. Workers share the cost: Quigley and Cardenas. Restructure the funds and pay ahead: Giannoulias and Mendoza. Change the state constitution: Wilson.</p>
<h2>In their words</h2>
<ul>
<li>Would put city bankruptcy on the table to deal with the pension debt. <q>We cannot borrow and spend our way out of this crisis.</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/chicago-mayor-election-candidates-2027-petitions-filed/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, Oct. 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Freeze the property tax levy in the first year, and no new taxes until Springfield reforms pensions. <q>I will freeze the property tax levy in my first year as mayor and demand structural pension reform from Springfield before any new tax increases are considered.</q> (<a href="https://johnkitover45.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign site</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<p>No spending report found for this campaign yet.</p>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>Not listed in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC).</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Would freeze the property tax levy in his first year, refuse new taxes until the state reforms pensions, cut borrowing, and consider bankruptcy for the pension debt.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>We cannot borrow and spend our way out of this crisis.</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/chicago-mayor-election-candidates-2027-petitions-filed/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, Oct. 2026</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>No position on record.</p>
